Measured: jsonlog costs nothing (karr #10). NOT measured: a DBI backend against a real PostgreSQL over TCP, which is a round-trip on the request path and can hang or fail.

Measure that first with bench/. Only if it costs real time, add a UsageStore::Buffered decorator around any store: bounded queue, flush on batch size or timer, flush on shutdown, JsonLog stays synchronous.

Overflow policy must be drop-and-count-loudly. Silently dropping billing data under a sink outage is the worst possible failure. Note the trade against ADR 0004: a crash loses one event today, up to N with buffering -- that is a config decision, not a default.

Separately and cheaply: when no usage store is configured at all, record_usage still builds the whole event before discovering there is no sink. Skip the work when disabled.

2026-09-10 01:43 Cheap part DONE (commit 076c05d): record_usage now returns the not-configured result before building/normalising the event when there is no sink at all. _has_usage_sink counts a store_usage_event callback, a usage store object, OR a subclass _store_usage_event override (a documented extension, t/14), so the skip is coderef-gated and does not swallow that case. New t/17-usage-record-skip.t.

UsageStore::Buffered NOT built, on purpose: this ticket is measurement-bound and the measurement it names -- a DBI backend against a real PostgreSQL over TCP -- was never taken. The 2026-08-15 bench round measured jsonlog only; the gateway-comparison report records that the bench config writes usage to no database store. jsonlog costs nothing (k10), so nothing on record justifies a bounded-buffer decorator. The heavier alternative (usage-WAL + separate writer process) exists only as an unadopted DRAFT spec, preserved in git stash 'skeid-wip-leiche-2026-09-10'. Leaving open: reopen the Buffered decision only after an actual remote-DBI-over-TCP measurement with bench/.

2026-09-15 17:12 MEASURED (2026-09-15): DBI usage store vs a REAL PostgreSQL over TCP DOES cost measurable
request-path time -- unlike jsonlog, which costs nothing (re-confirms k10). RECOMMENDATION:
