MECHANISM / INTERPRETATION
- The usage write runs AFTER request.finish (post-response), so it cannot delay the SAME
  request's first token. Confirmed: streaming TTFT is essentially FLAT (+4ms p50, tail within
  noise). The ticket's fear -- a request-path stall inflating TTFT -- did NOT materialise.
- The cost is a THROUGHPUT-CEILING drop (-19% stream / -28% json) + total-latency rise
  (+35..+52ms p50): each ~1.47ms synchronous fsync-insert blocks the single event loop and
  serialises all in-flight requests behind it. Driver = WAL fsync, not the TCP round-trip.

WHY NOT BUILD UsageStore::Buffered (drop-and-count-loudly, vs ADR 0004):
1. jsonlog is the documented default and pays ~nothing; the cost is DBI-over-TCP's alone, an
   opt-in blocking store (ADR 0004: "a store that can block must be opt-in").
2. It is a throughput ceiling, not a TTFT stall -> recoverable by PREFORK (ADR 0010: 1->4
   workers 128->170 req/s) which spreads the blocking write across N loops with ZERO durability
   cost. (Gap: DBI+prefork NOT measured here.)
3. Dominant cost is the per-commit fsync -- a postgres-side knob (synchronous_commit / group
   commit), not a Skeid code problem.
4. Buffered trades DURABLE BILLING DATA for latency: ADR 0004 accepts losing 1 event on crash;
   buffering makes it up to N. Justified only by a concrete deployment that is pinned to
   synchronous request-path postgres, cannot use jsonlog+out-of-band ingestion, has the
   throughput ceiling as its real bottleneck AND cannot add workers, and signs off on the
   crash-loss window. This measurement establishes none of those.

NUMBER THAT WOULD FLIP IT: a real deployment forced onto synchronous request-path postgres
where prefork+DB tuning cannot recover the throughput and the up-to-N crash-loss window is
acceptable. Absent that, keep jsonlog default; ship events to postgres out-of-band.

GAPS NOT MEASURED: single run per cell (no run-to-run variance; c=4 smoke agreed in
direction); workers=1 only (DBI+prefork = the recovery path, unmeasured); loopback TCP only (a
REMOTE/TLS postgres adds RTT to every blocking write -> makes DBI worse, strengthens "use
jsonlog"); empty->1010-row table (no large hot-table index/autovacuum cost); no
hang/connection-loss fault injection (a synchronous store that HANGS stalls the loop
indefinitely -- a reliability argument for jsonlog, orthogonal to latency); concurrency swept
at c=16 only.

